# § 1 Definitions, Stipulations & Specific Applications

### Original Source-Code Files
Individual files such as `.c`, `.py`, `.h`, `.ml`, configuration files, build scripts, etc., that constitute the project's internal architecture.

-   License: BSD 3-Clause
-   Notice: `© 2026 PHOVOS:[PHOVOS@outlook.com](mailto:PHOVOS@outlook.com)`
-   You may use, modify, and redistribute these individual source files (or derivatives thereof) freely under the terms of the BSD 3-Clause License.
-   When redistributing these source files or binaries you compile yourself from them, you must retain the BSD attribution and disclaimer.

### Original Source Code  

The original source code in this repository is licensed under the  
[BSD License](#bsd-license) (detailed in full below). If you are  
accessing this repository via a browser (e.g., without downloading  
it), this license still applies. For more information, visit:  
https://opensource.org/licenses/BSD-3-Clause  

### Distributed Code / Binaries / Images  

Any distributed code, binaries, or containerized images derived from  
this repository are licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution  
NonCommercial ShareAlike 4.0 International License ([CC BY-NC-SA 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/  
licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/)). To view a copy of this license, visit:  
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/

### Quine-Generated (dynamic) Code  

Any code generated by the quine functionality of this repository is  
explicitly dedicated to the public domain under the [CC0 License](  
https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/). This includes all  
outputs, rewrites, or transformations created through automated  
quine-based code generation. Users may use, modify, and distribute  
such content without restriction or attribution, except as limited by  
the terms of the CC0 license itself.

I recommend (but do not enforce) measures such as watermarking,  
sandboxing, and access controls to ensure responsible use of the  
generated code. However, I recognize that every project has unique  
requirements, and imposing arbitrary constraints would unnecessarily  
limit the flexibility of the code.

By choosing CC0, Morphological Source Code aims to foster innovation, encourage
reuse, and empower developers to make their own decisions about how to use and  
secure the code.

To-summarize:

- Original Code: Licensed under BSD  
- Distributed Code: Licensed under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, referencing BSD  
- Generated Code: Released to the public domain via CC0  

